  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\n\nFriday will see a stronger H5 shortwave eject from the Rockies \ntoward the western Dakotas. The sfc low will finally nudge to the \nnortheast, with the sfc warm front finally moving through the area. \nTemperatures should then warm to the mid to upper 80s. Behind the \nfront, will see another unstable air mass of 1,500 to 2,000 j/kg of \ninstability, and with some deep layer shear of around 30-35 kts, \ncould see a few storms become severe in the evening. Forecast \nsoundings show high LCLs, which would likely imply some wind hazard. \nPoPs remain at 50 to 70% for most of eastern Nebraska into southwest \nIowa.\n\nBy early Saturday morning, the main sfc low and cold front should \npush off to our southeast. Could still see some more showers and \nstorms along the frontal feature early Saturday morning (50-70% \nchance). Behind the front, most areas reach the low to mid 70s.\n\nSunday will see a more substantial H5 wave eject from Colorado \ntoward the area. The feature will help trigger or next round for \nrainfall, particularly across the southern half of the forecast \narea. Periodic chances for more rainfall continue through Tuesday as \nwe see more disturbances traverse the area, but chances remain\nlow at 15 to 30%.",
